  "paragraphs": [
    {
      "num": "01",
      "en": "Who is a {genius} ? This question has {greatly} interested {humankind} for {centuries} .",
      "cn": "谁是 <u> 天才 </u> ？几个 <u> 世纪 </u> 以来， <u> 人类 </u> 一直对这个问题 <u> 极为 </u> 感兴趣。"
    },
    {
      "num": "02",
      "en": "Let's state {clearly} : Einstein was a genius.His {face} is {almost} the {international} {symbol} for genius.But we want to go beyond one {man} and explore the {nature} of genius itself.Why is it that some people are so much more {intelligent} or creative than the {rest} of us? And who are they?",
      "cn": "让我们 <u> 明确 </u> 一点：爱因斯坦是个天才。他的 <u> 脸几乎 </u> 是天才的 <u> 国际象征 </u> 。但是，我们想超越某一个 <u> 人 </u> 去探讨天才本身的 <u> 本质 </u> 。为什么有些人比我们 <u> 其他人聪明 </u> 得多，或更有创造力？他们又是谁？"
    },
    {
      "num": "03",
      "en": "In the {sciences} and {arts} , those praised as geniuses were most often {white} men, of European {racial} origin. {Perhaps} this is not a surprise.It's said that history is written by the victors, and those victors set the {standards} for {admission} to the genius club.When {contributions} were made by geniuses outside the club—women, or {people} of a different {color} or belief—they were {unacknowledged} and {rejected} by others.Marie Curie, known for her research on {radium} and {radiation} , overcame significant challenges related to her gender.Despite societal norms valuing“ {Sir} ”over“ {Madam} ”，Curie's success shows how talent and perseverance can break barriers, inspiring future generations in science.",
      "cn": "在 <u> 科学 </u> 和 <u> 艺术 </u> 领域，那些被誉为天才的人往往是欧洲 <u> 种族 </u> 血统的 <u> 白人 </u> 男性。 <u> 也许 </u> 这并不令人惊讶。据说历史是由胜利者书写的，而也是那些胜利者制定了 <u> 进入 </u> 天才俱乐部的 <u> 标准 </u> 。当俱乐部外的天才——女性，或有不同 <u> 肤色 </u> 或信仰的 <u> 人 </u> ——做出 <u> 贡献 </u> 时，他们 <u> 不被认可 </u> ，甚至会遭到其他人的 <u> 排斥 </u> 。玛丽·居里因研究 <u> 镭 </u> 和 <u> 辐射 </u> 而闻名，她克服了与自身性别有关的重大挑战。尽管社会规范重“ <u> 先生 </u> ”而轻“ <u> 女士 </u> ”，但居里的成功表明，天赋和毅力可以突破阻碍，激励着科学领域的后辈。"
    },
    {
      "num": "04",
      "en": "A study {recently} published by Science found that as young as age six, {girls} are less likely than {boys} to say that members of their {gender} are “really, really {smart} .”Even worse, the study found that girls act on that belief: Around age six they start to avoid activities said to be for children who are “really, really smart”，while they would probably use this phrase to describe a {boy} .Can our planet afford to have any great {thinkers} become discouraged and give up? It doesn't take a genius to know the {answer} : absolutely not.",
      "cn": "《科学》杂志 <u> 最近 </u> 发表的一项研究发现，年仅六岁的 <u> 女孩 </u> 就比 <u> 男孩 </u> 更不太可能说自己 <u> 性别 </u> 的人“非常非常 <u> 聪明 </u> ”。更糟糕的是，该研究还发现女孩会按照这种观念行事：大约在六岁时，她们就开始避免参与那些被认为是“非常非常聪明”的孩子进行的活动，而她们可能会用这句话描述一个 <u> 男孩 </u> 。我们的星球能承受得起任何一位伟大的 <u> 思想家 </u> 感到气馁并放弃吗？即便不是天才也知道 <u> 答案 </u> ：绝对不能。"
    },
    {
      "num": "05",
      "en": "Here's the good {news} .In a {wired} world with {constant} {global} {communication} , we're all {positioned} to see flashes of genius wherever they appear.And the more we look, the more we will see that social factors like {sex} , {race} , and class do not determine the {appearance} of genius.As a writer says, future geniuses come from those with“ {intelligence} , {creativity} , {perseverance} , and simple good {fortune} , who are able to change the world.”",
      "cn": "这里有个好 <u> 消息 </u> 。在这个 <u> 全球 </u> 信息 <u> 不断交流 </u> 的互 <u> 联 </u> 世界里，我们都 <u> 能 </u> 看到出现在任何地方的天才的闪光点。而且我们观察得越多，就越会发现，诸如 <u> 性别 </u> 、 <u> 种族 </u> 和阶级等社会因素并不能决定天才的 <u> 出现 </u> 。正如一位作家所说，未来的天才会是那些拥有“ <u> 智慧 </u> 、 <u> 创造力 </u> 、 <u> 毅力 </u> 和纯粹的好 <u> 运气 </u> 的人，他们能够改变世界”。 MKqjwFu4arM4cgPc+LiB66EBg/AaE0u6XBr6IpfU5EiRRdX6Ad+uTD+Kdly4W5UG"
    }
  ],
